# Project Setup Guide (Supabase, Google Auth, & Razorpay)

Welcome to the Smart Chess project! This guide will walk you through setting up your own Supabase project, Google Authentication, and Razorpay account from scratch so the application is fully operational.

---

## 1. Create Your Supabase Project

First, you need to create the database that will power the application.

1. Go to [Supabase](https://supabase.com/) and create a free account (or log in).
2. Click **New Project**, select an organization, and give your project a name (e.g., "Smart Chess").
3. Create a strong database password and click **Create new project**. *(It may take a few minutes to provision).*
4. Once your dashboard loads, go to **Project Settings** (the gear icon at the bottom left) -> **API**.
5. Copy your **Project URL** and your **anon `public` API key**.
6. In the `Frontend` folder of this codebase, create a new file named `.env`.
7. Paste your credentials into the file exactly like this:
```env
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=your_project_url_here
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your_anon_key_here
```

---

## 2. Initialize the Database Tables

We need to create a table to store your users and their payment statuses. Supabase makes this easy using its SQL Editor.

1. In your Supabase Dashboard, click on **SQL Editor** in the left sidebar.
2. Click **New Query**.
3. Copy and paste the following SQL code into the editor:

```sql
-- Create a table to store user data and payment status
create table public.profiles (
  id uuid references auth.users not null primary key,
  email text,
  is_premium boolean default false,
  created_at timestamp with time zone default timezone('utc'::text, now()) not null
);

-- Turn on Row Level Security (RLS) so users can only access their own data
alter table public.profiles enable row level security;

create policy "Users can view own profile" 
on public.profiles for select using ( auth.uid() = id );

create policy "Users can update own profile" 
on public.profiles for update using ( auth.uid() = id );

-- Create a trigger to automatically create a profile when a new user signs up
create function public.handle_new_user() 
returns trigger as $$
begin
  insert into public.profiles (id, email)
  values (new.id, new.email);
  return new;
end;
$$ language plpgsql security definer;

create trigger on_auth_user_created
  after insert on auth.users
  for each row execute procedure public.handle_new_user();
```
4. Click **Run** at the bottom right. You should see a "Success" message.

